// R11 gap 2 (docs/OWNERSHIP.md, formerly "Still broken" table row 2, now a
// CLOSED paragraph): a struct holding an `arc` field was never dropped, so
// rule 4's retain (boxing the literal into `name`, `cell_arc_from_string` at
// the struct-literal field) leaked. `record` shapes were excluded from
// `hasDropCall` outright, so it held no matter what ownership the STRUCT
// binding carried. CLOSED 2026-09-15 (late night): `codegen.zig` now emits
// `cell_drop_Session(cell_Session *r)` glue after the typedef and releases
// `sess` through it at the end of every iteration, so this measures 0 on
// both witnesses (it was 3000 on both, through the same host and counter,
// on the tree before the glue). It stays in the gate BECAUSE it is closed:
// pinned at 0, any nonzero reading re-opens row 2.
//
// This fixture avoids binding a separate `arc` local: `name` is boxed
// directly from a string literal in the struct literal (BOX, R11 retain
// rule 1). An `arc` local bound inside this same `while` body before being
// stored would ALSO trip the block-scoped-local gap
// (examples/leaks/block_scoped_local.cell).
//
// **IT DOES NOT ISOLATE THE STRUCT-FIELD GAP, AND AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS
// COMMENT CLAIMED IT DID.** That claim said `sess` is "an `owned` RECORD, not
// an `arc` local, so the block-scoped gap (which is specific to
// `arc`-annotated bindings) does not apply to it". Measured: the block-scoped
// gap is NOT specific to `arc`. `emitStmts` opens with
//
//     const mark = self.locals.items.len;
//     defer self.locals.shrinkRetainingCapacity(mark);
//
// and that pop reads no annotation at all. It removes a record exactly as it
// removes an `arc` local, so `sess` is off `self.locals` before the `while`
// statement finishes and the function-scoped `emitScopeDrops` never sees it.
// The emitted C declares `sess` inside the loop braces, where it is not
// nameable at function end either. A record local in a `while` body gets no
// drop emitted at all today; that was checked by emitting one.
//
// **The consequence for anyone about to build per-struct drop glue: it will
// move this fixture's number by exactly ZERO**, because the local is gone
// before any function-scoped drop point runs. Moving it additionally requires
// a block-scoped drop point, which is R11 row 4's work. Write a
// FUNCTION-scoped fixture and pin that instead; do not "fix" this one by
// hoisting the local out of the loop, which would be loosening a measurement
// to make a number move.
//
// The comment being corrected here enumerated the ANNOTATIONS the block gap
// had been observed on and asserted a property of every binding, which is the
// same reasoning failure `AGENTS.md` tracks -- in a file whose entire purpose
// is to measure rather than assert.
//
// Status: parses, passes `cell check`. LLVM and MLIR refuse it (arc is
// scalar-first-refused there, same as examples/arc.cell); lives under
// examples/leaks/ so it is invisible to the top-level examples/*.cell
// corpus, agreement, and mlir-lowering loops (see examples/leaks/README.md).

pub struct Session {
    arc name: String
    copy id: Int
}

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn main() {
    var i = 0
    while i < 1000 {
        let owned sess = Session { name: "session", id: i }
        i = i + 1
    }
    print_int(i)
}
